I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says, “an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own,” and that the object scored is “the last top-level object in the text” when no fence holds one. Reply one is the exact text `{"a":1} {"text": "{"b":2}", "extra": 3}`. Reply two is the exact text `{"a":2} {"text": "{"b":2}", "extra": 3}`. The rule requires `{"a":1}` for reply one and `{"a":2}` for reply two, because the nested `{"b":2}` is inside the invalid outer object and is not a candidate, so the last top-level object is the first object in each reply. The check, however, returns `{"b":2}` for both replies: the raw_decode on the invalid outer object fails, the cursor is not advanced past it, and the scan then reaches the nested `{` and succeeds on `{"b":2}`, appending it as a top-level candidate; since the pool is scanned in reverse, that nested object is returned. I would be wrong if the check returns different answers for the two replies, for example `{"a":1}` for reply one and `{"a":2}` for reply two, or if it raises on either.